Final Project - Operating Systems Course

Part_1 explanation - 
Node.hpp - Node class which will represent the nodes in our Graph object.
The Node has an id and a neighbors vector that contains the pair data structure, that consists of a <id of the neighbor, the weight of the edge connected between>.
we have also implemented a couple of getters and setters in order for it to function correctly.

Graph.hpp|cpp - Graph class that stores the nodes vector, supports add/remove vertex + edges, keeps the graph directed/undirected flag, and exposes helper methods (degree, indegree, contains_edge, print operator) that we reuse in the rest of the project.


Part_2 explanation -
Euler_circle_algo.hpp|cpp - we implement Hierholzer’s algorithm for our Graph. 
there are function helpers that check even degree, connectivity and directed inout degree balance so we know if an Euler cycle exists (required for euler circles). 
find_euler_circle_string() is the string building function helper that prints the circuit or if one does not exist then it returns - “No Eulerian cycle”.

makefile + covertage report + profile report + valgrind memcheck report + valgrind callgrind report

Part_3 explanation -
random_graph.hpp|cpp -  uses getopt to read -v / -e / -s / (-d/-u) flags, generates a random Graph with optional direction,
prints it, and then reuses find_euler_circle_string(). 
helper functions compute the possible edge sets for both directed and undirected graphs
Euler_circle_algo.cpp|hpp - same algo from the last part.

coverage_report.txt that contains multiple runs of invalid inputs, exceptions, algorithm functionality etc.. 
gprof_report.txt that contains a an example of a graph that we build in the program and run the euler algo on it for 1000000 times to see what part takes up most of the process in the program as we did in previos assignments.

valgrind_output.txt - a single run of valgrind memcheck that checks for any leaks in the memory.

valgrind_call_output.txt - a single run of valgrind callgrind

Part_6 explanation -
graph_server.cpp - first network server version. we keep a select() loop that multiplexes stdin + all client sockets, allowing shutdown (by typing "QUIT") from the console in the server, and let each client create a graph, push edges, and ask for EULER. per-client state (Graph pointer, direction flags, edge-insert guard) is tracked in unordered_maps keyed by the socket fd.

Euler_circle_algo.cpp|hpp - a copy of the last parts.. these files contain the euler circuit finder function and find_euler_circle_string returns the string presentation of the circuit.

makefile plus coverage/valgrind/callgrind reports included.

Part_7 explanation -
graph_server.cpp - we evolved the server: same select() from part 6 but now we give the client more commands (HAM, MSTW, SCC, CLIQUE). 
AlgorithmFactory gives us back the chosen algorithm object, and we block invalid requests..
we still guard client state (graph_created, edges_inserted, direction) so each session stays coherent.

Algorithms/ AlgorithmsFactory - Factory (EulerCircuit, HamCircuit, MSTW, SCC, MaxClique). each execute() implementation returns the printable answer we send back to the client.

NOTE - some of the algorithms may or may not work on the the graph since some only work on directed graphs while others only work on undirected (we inform this to the client)

random_graph.cpp|hpp - file that contains a generate graph function for both directed|undirected graphs.

makefile plus coverage/callgrind/valgrind-memcheck reports included.

Part_8 explanation -
graph_server.cpp - we swapped select() for a leader follower pool built with pthreads. workers coordinate with lf_mx/lf_cv so only one accepts at a time, but they all help process requests.
we also added RandomGraph command that connects to random_graph.cpp. 
calculate_and_send() now runs every supported algorithm for the current built graph and streams the results back to the client in one go, skipping the ones that don’t fit the graph direction. 
shutdown flags + active_clients vector let QUIT broadcast a clean logout.
random_graph.hpp|cpp - refactored to live next to the server and generate graphs on demand. 

profiling/helgrind/valgrind reports show the synchronization fixes we validated in this part.

Part_9 explanation -
Pipeline_Pattern.hpp|cpp + ActiveGraph.cpp|hpp - final concurrency upgrade. 
ActiveGraph is a tiny active object with its own worker thread and a job queue. 
Pipeline strings five of these stages together so each graph request flows through dedicated workers. push_to() hops results from one stage to the next.
graph_server.cpp - server side now builds a Pipeline per client session. each stage runs one algorithm (Euler -> Hamiltonian -> MSTW -> SCC -> Max Clique) and skips unsupported ones with a friendly message. 
safe_send() guards writes per client so interleaving threads do not corrupt output. shutdown handling mirrors Part_8 but the heavy lifting is done inside the pipeline.
random_graph.cpp|hpp (same as last part)


*NOTE* - parts 2,3,6,7,8,9 all have their required tests (memcheck,profiling, callgrind, helgrind, coverage)